      caption="Port identities and UN/LOCODEs are HIGH confidence from the destination data. Free-time and congestion notes are LOW confidence where not in the ACL Hamburg tariff."
    />
    <SourceList sources={portSources} title="Sources — European destination ports" />

    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>Gateway-specific demurrage stories:</strong> Felixstowe is the
      post-Brexit clearance story — complete the UK declaration and GB EORI before
      arrival to avoid a hold. Genoa needs a strike/congestion buffer. Rotterdam
      and Antwerp barge delays have stretched past 72 hours in 2026, so inland
      handoff can be the hidden delay even after the box leaves the quay.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>5. Cost composition: the full destination-charge stack</h2>
      <p>
        Demurrage is only one line on the destination invoice. The full stack also
        includes detention, THC, port service fees, inspection risk, storage and
        ISPS — most of which were not quantified in the verified snapshot. Do not
        accept a quote that hides these in an "all-in" lump sum.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'layer', label: 'Cost component' },
          { key: 'charge', label: 'Indicative magnitude' },
          { key: 'note', label: 'Planning note' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={costRows}
        caption="The ACL Hamburg demurrage, EU duty and country VAT are MEDIUM confidence. Unquantified destination charges are LOW confidence and must be requested as an itemised schedule."
      />
      <SourceList sources={costSources} title="Sources — destination cost composition" />

      <p class="mt-2">
        The daily arithmetic is unforgiving: a 40ft dry box held past free time in
        Hamburg costs <strong>115 EUR/day for days 1–7</strong>, then
        <strong>180 EUR/day from day 8</strong>. A single customs hold can therefore
        consume hundreds of euros before the container even leaves the terminal,
        and detention can add another clock after pickup.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>6. Compliance: clear before arrival to avoid the clock</h2>

    <h3>Pre-arrival document readiness</h3>
    <p>
      The single best demurrage control is to complete clearance before the vessel
      arrives. Have the <strong>EORI number</strong> (or <strong>GB EORI</strong>
      for the UK) registered, the <strong>T1 transit document</strong> filed where
      applicable, and the commercial invoice, packing list, TARIC/HS classification
      and customs declaration ready. Pre-book the truck and empty return before
      discharge so paperwork does not consume the free days.
    </p>

    <h3>EU duty and VAT</h3>
    <p>
      Duty follows the <strong>EU Common Customs Tariff</strong>, typically
      <strong>0–12%</strong> by HS/TARIC code, with no China–EU free-trade agreement
      in force. Import VAT is then charged on the duty-inclusive value at the
      destination-country rate.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'country', label: 'Country' },
        { key: 'vat', label: 'Standard VAT' },
        { key: 'note', label: 'Note' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={vatRows}
      caption="Country VAT rates are MEDIUM confidence from the destination data. The UK is a separate post-Brexit regime."
    />
    <SourceList sources={complianceSources} title="Sources — EU/UK duty, VAT and EORI" />

    <h3>CE marking and REACH</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>CE marking</strong> applies to many regulated products and
      <strong>REACH</strong> regulates chemical substances. Product-conformity
      holds can delay release just like missing customs documents, so confirm
      applicability and prepare the technical file before shipment.
    </p>

    <h3>FASAH/SABER and GCC rules do not apply</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>FASAH and SABER are Saudi/GCC systems</strong>, and the
      <strong>GCC 5% customs baseline</strong>, Saudi 15% VAT and UAE 5% VAT are
      Gulf-specific. They do not apply to the EU or the UK and should not be
      copied into a China-to-Europe demurrage-prevention workflow. Europe uses
      EORI/GB EORI, the EU Common Customs Tariff, destination-country VAT, CE
      marking and REACH instead.
    </p>
  </section>

